{:edb/db-before-t 2122 :edb/db-after-t 2123 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535435 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:02:53.351Z" 13194139535435 true] [17592186229137 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349017 13194139535435 true] [17592186229137 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349021 13194139535435 true] [17592186229138 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349024 13194139535435 true] [17592186229139 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349028 13194139535435 true] [17592186229579 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 4x + 2$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535435 false] [17592186229579 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4x+2.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535435 true] [17592186229579 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x.\n$$" 13194139535435 true] [17592186229579 1145 "Try $y_p=ax+b$. Substitution into $y^{\\prime}+2y=4x+2$ gives $2ax+(a+2b)=4x+2$, so $a=2$ and $b=0$. Thus $y_p=2x$. Adding the supplied complementary solution gives $y(x)=Ae^{-2x}+2x$." 13194139535435 false] [17592186349017 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349018 13194139535435 true] [17592186349017 1089 "q-332292" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349017 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535435 true] [17592186349017 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349017 1113 #uuid "5ac3284f-adb8-5c84-8842-ce33dc9e9c4d" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349017 1145 "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha =-10\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5.\n$$" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349019 13194139535435 true] [17592186349018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349020 13194139535435 true] [17592186349018 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535435 true] [17592186349018 1041 "field-1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349018 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349020 13194139535435 true] [17592186349018 1110 #uuid "6a05bd5a-2fc8-5178-bca4-d955ae19c687" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349019 1034 "-5" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535435 true] [17592186349019 1111 #uuid "b80ac86f-4ee0-5a4b-be80-3ed623d8c1e8" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349020 1034 "5" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535435 true] [17592186349020 1111 #uuid "e21e6871-9319-5a36-9bc9-3d9b60ab02ab" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349021 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349022 13194139535435 true] [17592186349021 1089 "q-332307" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349021 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535435 true] [17592186349021 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349021 1113 #uuid "bf6e3127-f9d0-53df-9d6d-0255fcb68d4b" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349021 1145 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349023 13194139535435 true] [17592186349022 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535435 true] [17592186349022 1041 "field-1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349022 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349023 13194139535435 true] [17592186349022 1110 #uuid "b11e040d-97c7-5771-b1d3-2a5f4f468246" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349023 1034 "-x-1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535435 true] [17592186349023 1111 #uuid "729f2eee-1524-5df7-86de-567f97f434c1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349024 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349025 13194139535435 true] [17592186349024 1089 "q-332539" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349024 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535435 true] [17592186349024 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=5x-4.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349024 1113 #uuid "2f3493ad-3f34-5e86-8757-be73d8ebaba4" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349024 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\alpha x+(\\alpha +5\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +5\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+x-1.\n$$" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349026 13194139535435 true] [17592186349025 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349027 13194139535435 true] [17592186349025 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535435 true] [17592186349025 1041 "field-1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349025 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349027 13194139535435 true] [17592186349025 1110 #uuid "55924886-94d9-5faa-aed2-a5e64cb8dd9b" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349026 1034 "Ae^{-5x}+x+1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535435 true] [17592186349026 1111 #uuid "a41db1db-77de-5d53-a5a9-4f3699888aa0" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349027 1034 "Ae^{-5x}+x-1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535435 true] [17592186349027 1111 #uuid "565f4ebb-393e-543c-a2e5-4d7204aa5746" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349028 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349029 13194139535435 true] [17592186349028 1089 "q-333042" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535435 true] [17592186349028 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem." 13194139535435 true] [17592186349028 1113 #uuid "53a56fd0-2d25-5ed0-9417-d9b97a813796" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349028 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -4\\beta )x+(\\beta -4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -4\\beta =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -4\\gamma =-3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349030 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349031 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349032 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349033 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349034 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1041 "selection" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349032 13194139535435 true] [17592186349029 1110 #uuid "fe0a4f29-2bef-5a56-913a-703b8badacaa" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349030 1034 "y=-2{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+1" 13194139535435 true] [17592186349030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535435 